From a5efc15fac3dcd04ad1b2c77e4db5d656fea03df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 00:17:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] media: iris: ROG5: contain fatal firmware errors instead
 of reloading

On the ROG Phone 5 (SM8350, OEM vpu20_4v firmware) every decode session
ended with a firmware fatal error (k114 trial). The driver then reloaded
the firmware from iris_sys_error_handler() while the instances stayed on
the list with their old session ids: the reloaded firmware answered every
later command with HFI_ERR_SESSION_INVALID_SESSION_ID (0x1004), waiters
were never woken (later sessions hung in poll), the cycle repeated, and
the phone hard-hung four minutes after the last fatal error.

- A system error (dispatched by event type, whatever session id it
  carries) or a firmware watchdog interrupt latches core->fatal for the
  rest of this driver binding. iris_core_init() then fails with -EIO and
  every command is refused; the error is contained from the work item
  instead of reloading: every instance is set to ERROR, its vb2 queues are
  errored and its completions woken (each under its own lock; never
  inst->lock inside core->lock), then the firmware is shut down through
  TrustZone. Only if that shutdown succeeds is the core powered off and the
  shared memory freed; otherwise clocks, votes and memory stay (fw_held)
  and the core is never reused. This also applies to iris_core_deinit().
- Session commands go through iris_hfi_queue_session_cmd_write(), which
  refuses them unless the session was opened on the firmware instance that
  runs now (same core init attempt, no fatal error, core in INIT).
- iris_wait_for_session_response() returns -EIO when the answer carried
  an error (the handler set the instance to ERROR before completing) or
  the wait was ended by a fatal error.
- An event for an unknown session is logged, not treated as a system
  error.
- Interrupt and power ordering: iris_vpu_power_off() disables the IRQ
  before the clocks go instead of after; the runtime PM callbacks quiesce
  the IRQ outside core->lock like the teardown paths (the threaded
  handler takes that lock and disable_irq() waits for it); the threaded
  handler and the command path touch VPU registers only while
  core->powered. NOC LPI handshake timeouts during power off are logged.
- iris_remove() cancels the error work before tearing down.

The driver must be rebound (modprobe -r qcom_iris; modprobe qcom_iris) to
use the video core again after a fatal error.

ROG5: patch 0159, kernel k115. Not in upstream.
